    "story": "The window has been her post all morning, and she has not moved from it since the light came up. The jars on the sill have not moved either — they are the same ones as yesterday, and the day before that, and the day before that. She is not looking at anything you could point to; she is looking at the road past the end of the street, where the dust has not risen yet and the trucks have not come. Somewhere out there, the men she is waiting for are deciding whether today is the day they come home. She already knows what she will do when she sees them. For now, she waits, and she has learned to wait well.",
    "notes": "This is the home front in a single frame. A woman leaning out of her kitchen window, jars on the sill behind her, watching a road that has not brought her men home yet — the painterly black and white turns a plain moment of waiting into a portrait of devotion. Collectors keep coming back to this one because it is the quietest kind of longing, and the quietest kind is the one that stays with you.",
