    "story": "The dust is the first thing he notices every day, and the last thing he forgets at night. It gets into the webbing, into the creases of the battledress, into the corners of his eyes, and it stays there — a fine, pale powder that makes him feel as if he has been walking through the inside of a photograph. He has been. He has been walking since before the light turned good, and he will be walking long after it goes. The rifle on his shoulder has not changed its weight all day, though it has felt heavier with every mile. Somewhere ahead, there is a place to stop. He will know it when he sees the smoke of it.",
    "notes": "This is the portrait that carries a whole march in a single frame. A British Tommy in his Brodie helmet, rifle on his shoulder, walking through dust that has settled into every crease of his battledress — the painterly black and white gives a reenactment the gravity of a period photograph of a man who has been walking all day. It is a frame about endurance, and collectors who want a portrait that stands for something will find it here.",
