    "story": "The smile is small and it does not reach his eyes, and that is how you know it is a real one. He has stopped on the path because the column has stopped, and the column has stopped because somebody ahead has found something worth stopping for, and he does not need to know what it is. His hands rest where they have rested all day — on the rifle, on the webbing, on the map case that has already been consulted and refolded twice. The field stretches out in every direction, and the light is going the way it goes at the end of a long day. He will walk it in the dark if he has to. He has walked it in the dark before.",
    "notes": "This is the portrait that carries the whole march in one frame. A young soldier on a dirt path, rifle in hand, map case on his hip, smiling the smallest of smiles at the end of a long day — the painterly black and white turns a reenactment into a period photograph of a man who has been walking since morning. It is the kind of frame that reminds a collector that the war was mostly walking, and the walking was the hard part.",
