    "story": "She keeps the white band on her arm clean the way a medic keeps her kit clean, because a man on the ground has to be able to see it from where he fell. It is what tells a man lying in the dirt that she is coming for him and not past him, and she knows the weight of that promise. Her hand rests on her hip, not from impatience but from the habit of a woman who has stood in this same spot a hundred times, watching the road for the next stretcher, the next call, the next boy who needs her to be steady. She is not looking at the camera. She is looking at the place where the next hurt is going to come from, and she is already going there in her head.",
    "notes": "This is the medic portrait that a buyer remembers after leaving the wall. A woman in a U.S. Army uniform with the red cross band on her sleeve, hand on her hip, eyes on a road the camera cannot see — the painterly black and white gives her readiness the gravity of a promise. Collectors value frames like this because they are not about a pose; they are about the woman who walked toward the wounded, and the band that told the wounded she could be trusted.",
