    "story": "She does not look up. Looking up would mean seeing the line of stretchers still waiting outside the tent, and the line is longer than the morning. So she keeps her eyes on this one man, on the rise and fall of his chest under her hands, on the small sounds a body makes when it is deciding whether to hold on. The stethoscope hangs cold against her apron until she lifts it, and then it is warm from her own skin. Somewhere beyond the canvas, a generator hums and somebody calls for more bandages. She hears it all and answers none of it yet — this man, this minute, is the whole of her work.",
    "notes": "A rare, tender frame of frontline nursing: the nurse bent to her work, the stethoscope catching the light, the wounded man resting half in shadow at the edge of the frame. The composition pulls the viewer into the moment of care rather than the fact of war, and the painterly softness of the light makes it one of the most human portraits in the series. A quietly powerful piece for any collection of World War II or Facing History photography.",
