    "story": "The apron is still damp from the morning's washing, and she has not taken it off. There was no time — the street filled with uniforms before the bread was out of the oven, and she came out as she was, flour on her hands and all. Now a soldier stands with his back to the camera in front of her, and another leans in from behind, and she is laughing at something one of them said, a joke she only half understands. It does not matter. Four years of watching the road, of keeping her head down and her voice low, and today the road brought her sons home — not her own sons, but somebody's, and that is enough. She will remember this morning every time she ties the apron.",
